Messages:

    Messages  always consist of a primary message which may be a
"self-msg"  in the case of functions and not actually be present
in the message expression.  The primary message maybe optionally
be  followed  by  parameters  and  message  delimiters  and then
possibly a message terminator.  

Semantics of Parameter Evaluation:

    Parameters   have   two  forms  evaluated  and  unevaluated.
Evaluated  parameters  are  specified by a '$' and are evaluated
before  being passed.  Unevaluated parameters are specified by a
'&'  and  signal the method that it is a macro.  The unevaluated
parameter's  parsed text representation is a DALE expression and
is  passed  to  the  macro  as  a  DALE  expression object.  The
evaluated  parameters  if  any  can  not be referenced until the
macro  is expanded and evaluated.  Their names maybe manipulated
by the body of the macro.  The unevaluated parameter expressions
maybe  referenced and  manipulated by the macro body.  The macro
must  build  a DALE block or expression object and when finished
the  block  or  expression  is  evaluated  in the context of the
expression where it was expanded.

Semantics of the Assignment Message:

    Assignment  ('<-') is the only special message in dale.  The
left  hand  side of a assignment will be referred to as a lvalue
or  lexpression.    Only  instance variables, block variables or
lexpressions consisting of a instance or block variable followed
by a message expression may be assigned to.  Parameters can not 
be altered  or  used  as lvalues or lexpressions.  Assignment is
done by sending the message _asign to the variable's object.  In
the  case  of  lexpressions  the primary message is concatenated
with  '_asign' to produce a message that signals a assignment is
pending and to not return a new resultant object but to return a
reference to where that assignment is to take place.

Message Evaluation:

    Message  expressions  are  evaluated  in  the context of the
receiving object's message template.  Each method has a template
associated  with  it  that  is uniquely specified by the primary
message.    The receiving object will be referred as the subject
of  the expression and is always present and the first object in
a expression.    
    Evaluation of expressions is done in a left to right fashion
using the following rules:  

If  the  expression  contains  only one item then that object is
returned.  otherwise:

Get  the  template  for  the  primary  message from the subjects
object's class.

If the template consists of only a primary message then
    Execute the block for that method and 
    Substitute the returned object for the old subject and
      primary message
    Return the evaluated reduced expression.

If the template consists of a primary message followed by
  a parameter then
    if the item following the primary message is an expression
        then evaluate the expression and replace it with the
          returned object.
    Push the object following the primary message on the stack 
      as a parameter and 
    Execute the block for the subject's method and 
    Substitute the returned object for the new subject of the 
      rest of the expression if any
    Return the evaluated reduced expression.

Note:  if at this point the template consists of message 
  delimiters and/or maybe a terminator.

If the method for the subject and primary message is NOT a 
  macro then 
    Set prevmsg to the primary message. 
    Set current_delimiter to the first delimiter or terminator. 
    While current_delimiter exists 
        if  item  following  prevmsg  is  current_delimiter then
            signal an error has occured.
cont:   if the next item is current_delimiter then
            item following prevmsg is a parameter.
        else 
            evaluate_single_expr with item following prevmsg as
              the subject.
            goto cont.
        Set prevmsg to current_delimiter.
        Set current_delimiter to the next delimiter.
    End while 
    if prevmsg is not a terminator then
        if the item following prevmsg is an expression then
            evaluate the expression and replace it with the
              returned object.
        Push the object following prevmsg on the stack
          as a parameter and
    Execute the block for the subject's method and
    Substitute the returned object for the new subject of the
      rest of the expression if any and
    Return the evaluated reduced expression.

Note: if at this point the method is a macro.

Set  prevmsg  to  the primary message.
Set current_delimiter to the  first  delimiter  or  terminator.
While current_delimiter exists
    if template parameter following prevmsg is an unevaluated
      parameter then
        Place all items between prevmsg and current_delimiter
          in an expression object and
        Push it on the stack as a parameter.
    else
        if  item  following  prevmsg  is  current_delimiter then
            signal an error has occured.
cont2:  if the next item is current_delimiter then
            item following prevmsg is a parameter.
        else
            evaluate_single_expr with item following prevmsg as
              the subject.
            goto cont2.
    Set prevmsg to current_delimiter.
    Set current_delimiter to the next delimiter.
End while
  if prevmsg is not a terminator then
      if the template's parameter following prevmsg is an
        unevaluated parameter then 
            all items following prevmsg to the end of the
            expression are placed in a expression object and
            the object is put on the stack as a parameter.
      else
          if the item following prevmsg is an expression then 
              evaluate the expression and replace it with the 
                returned object. 
          Push the object following prevmsg on the stack
            as a parameter.
  Execute the block for the subject's macro method checking
    that all evaluated parameter are not referenced.
  With the returned block or expression from the macro
      Resolve all evaluated parameter references.
      Replace the macro call with the returned expression
  Return the reevaluated expression.

End of expression evaluation.

evaluate_single_expr
    This is nearly the same as expression evaluation except that
after   one  reduction  the  routine  returns  with  the  single
reduction  as  the  subject  of  the  rest  of  the  unevaluated
expression so the caller can continue reduction on it.
    Execute  is also similar except that storage is reserved for
block variables and each expression in the block is evaluated.

Semantics of Variable Types:

    All  untyped variables default to type 'object'.  The object
type  is a pointer to an object.  A typed variable is equivalent
to  assigning  the variable a new instance of that type or class
and  then  performing  a type check each time it is assigned to.
All  untyped  parameters  default  to  type  'object'.  The type
object  includes all other types.  A typed parameter does a type
check  to  insure  that  the parameter passed to it really is of
that type or class.

Diphthongs:

    Diphthongs  are  symbols that are a special character or two
special  characters.  The are special in the sense that they can
not  be part of a variable name of keyword.  When a diphthong is
recognized  it  is  surrounded by white space before the text is
parsed.  The predefined diphthongs are '(',')',';','&','$','($','$)' and '<-'.  The user may add more diphthongs such a '+' or '
++' by using the '#diphthong directive'.

Labels:

    Labels referred to in a goto statement are searched from the
innermost block where the goto occurred to the outermost block.

User Notes on Macros:

    If a macro might contain nested macros be sure that the only
place  where  a nested macro could be valid is inside of a block
or parenthesized expression.
    A  nonterminated  unevaluated parameter is terminated by the
end of expression in which the call is in.

Special Notes:

    It  might  be  a good idea not to require a ';' after blocks
that are  at  the  end of a expression ie, '$)' and '$);' should
both  be  valid.    Also  type names name may also be changed to
allow message expressions to follow them.
